    <statement><p>Find the intersection of the line and plane:</p>
<p><m>2x+2y+4z=-2, \qquad \mathbf{r}(t)=\left\lt 2,-3,0\right&gt;+t\left\lt -3,-2,3\right&gt;</m></p>
<p><m>P=(</m> <fillin characters="1" name="AnSwEr0001"/>,<fillin characters="1" name="AnSwEr0002"/>,<fillin characters="1" name="AnSwEr0003"/><m>)</m></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-3</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x=2-3t,\qquad y=-\left(3+2t\right),\qquad z=3t</m>



We substitute the parametric equations of the plane and solve for <m>t</m>, to find the 
value of <m>t</m> for which <m>\left(x,y,z\right)</m>  lies on the plane. We obtain:



<m>2x+2y+4z=-2</m>



<m>2\mathopen{}\left(2-3t\right)+2\mathopen{}\left(-\left(3+2t\right)\right)+4\cdot 3t=-2</m>



<m>t=0</m>



We find the coordinates of the point <m>P</m> of intersection by substituting <m>t=0</m> 
in the parametric equations of the line. We obtain:



<m>x=2+0\cdot (-3)=2 \qquad y=-3+0\cdot (-2)=-3 \qquad z=0+0\cdot (3)=0</m>



That is



<m>P=\left( 2,-3,0 \right)</m>




</solution>
